1. The Dual N-Back Study — Susanne M. Jaeggi (2008)

Researchers trained participants using a dual n-back task, where they had to remember both sounds and visual positions simultaneously. As they improved, the task became more difficult.

• Participants who trained for longer showed larger gains on tests of fluid intelligence. The study suggested that challenging working memory might improve certain reasoning abilities, although later research has produced mixed findings.

2. The Abecedarian Project — Craig Ramey

Researchers provided young children with years of structured learning, language activities, and cognitive stimulation beginning in infancy, while another group did not receive the same intensive program.

• The intervention group generally scored higher on cognitive tests and achieved better educational outcomes later in life. The study highlighted how a stimulating early environment can strongly influence intellectual development.

3. The Flynn Effect — James R. Flynn

James Flynn analyzed IQ scores collected over many decades and found that average test scores had risen across multiple generations in many countries.

• He argued that improvements in education, nutrition, healthcare, and increasingly complex environments likely contributed to these gains, showing that measured cognitive performance is influenced by more than genetics alone.
